#: .landing.hero.eyebrow
msgid "Arch Linux · Configuration as data · {{release}}"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.hero.title
msgid "An installer that <em>knows what it’s doing.</em>"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.hero.lede
msgid "Vanilla Arch underneath. A configuration knowledge base on top — every setting, every interaction between settings, transparently encoded."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.cta.download
msgid "Download ISO"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.cta.source
msgid "Source on GitHub"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.principles.eyebrow
msgid "What makes it different"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.principles.title
msgid "Three principles, applied throughout."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.principles.transparent.num
msgid "01 — TRANSPARENT"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.principles.transparent.title
msgid "Every choice is data"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.principles.transparent.body
msgid "Settings, dependencies, and lifecycle scripts live in a KDL knowledge base — auditable, forkable, fetched at install time so improvements ship without re-spinning an ISO."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.principles.hardened.num
msgid "02 — HARDENED"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.principles.hardened.title
msgid "Security by default"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.principles.hardened.body
msgid "Per-CPU vulnerability mitigation, system hardening sysctls, restricted user namespaces, optional hardened_malloc. Opt-out, not opt-in — and every trade-off is explained where you make it."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.principles.desktops.num
msgid "03 — FOUR DESKTOPS"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.principles.desktops.title
msgid "XFCE · Wayfire · Niri · COSMIC"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.principles.desktops.body
msgid "Pick one, or install all four side by side and switch from the greeter. Wayfire and Niri come with waybar, nwg-launchers and nwgbar already configured; XFCE and COSMIC bring their own panels. None of them is a stripped-down minimum."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.desktops.eyebrow
msgid "Choose, mix, switch"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.desktops.title
msgid "Four equal desktops, not one default and three afterthoughts."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.desktops.lede
msgid "Wayfire and Niri are commonly considered incomplete environments. Ditana ships everything they need — a polished waybar, nwg-launcher, nwgbar, and a consistent panel layout — and treats all four desktops as first-class citizens."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.desktops.xfce.alt
msgid "XFCE on Ditana with Blue Flat theme, Firefox, Thunar"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.desktops.xfce.caption
msgid "<strong>XFCE</strong> — Xorg, mature, fast"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.desktops.wayfire.alt
msgid "Wayfire on Ditana with Blue Vibrant theme, Brave, Thunar"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.desktops.wayfire.caption
msgid "<strong>Wayfire</strong> — Wayland, Compiz-inspired"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.desktops.niri.alt
msgid "Niri on Ditana with Blue Vibrant theme, Brave, COSMIC Files"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.desktops.niri.caption
msgid "<strong>Niri</strong> — Wayland, scrollable tiling"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.desktops.cosmic.alt
msgid "COSMIC on Ditana with Amber Rounded theme, LibreWolf, Thunar"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.desktops.cosmic.caption
msgid "<strong>COSMIC</strong> — Wayland, System76’s Rust desktop"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.snapshots.eyebrow
msgid "Rolling release, without the risk"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.snapshots.title
msgid "Every upgrade is reversible."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.snapshots.lede
msgid "Arch’s rolling-release model is a feature, not a bug — but it can land breakage on a Tuesday morning. Ditana takes the sting out by snapshotting the system before every upgrade, automatically."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.snapshots.subtitle
msgid "Atomic snapshots, taken by a pacman hook."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.snapshots.alt
msgid "System maintenance dialog showing automatic system snapshots option enabled by default"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.snapshots.p1
msgid "On ZFS and BTRFS, snapshots are atomic and free — a few milliseconds before any package upgrade. On XFS and EXT4, Timeshift handles the same job via rsync, completing typical incremental snapshots in seconds."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.snapshots.p2
msgid "The first snapshot is created by the installer itself, just before the first reboot. That means you can roll back to a freshly-installed system from day one — no setup, no first-upgrade chicken-and-egg."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.snapshots.p3
msgid "System snapshots, not data snapshots: <code>/home</code> and other data volumes are never touched. Roll back the system; keep your work."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.snapshots.p4
msgid "For ZFS, Ditana ships its own <a href=\"https://github.com/acrion/zfs-autosnap\">zfs-autosnap</a> fork that — unlike the upstream — removes the pacman lock file from the snapshot, so a rollback leaves you with a working pacman immediately."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.headless.title
msgid "Headless too."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.headless.body
msgid "System hardening plus automatic snapshots make Ditana a viable Arch-based server choice — uncommon for a rolling release. The installer’s <em>Server</em> profile skips the desktop install entirely and leans into the security defaults."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.hardware.eyebrow
msgid "Hardware-aware"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.hardware.title
msgid "It checks the upstream sources at install time."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.hardware.nvidia.alt
msgid "NVIDIA driver decision dialog with detected PCI ID and recommendation rationale"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.hardware.nvidia.title
msgid "NVIDIA detection, four-way decision."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.hardware.nvidia.p1
msgid "Ditana queries the live PCI ID lists from <code>github.com/NVIDIA/open-gpu-kernel-modules</code> and the official legacy GPU page at install time. Based on the result, it recommends one of four driver options – nvidia-open-dkms, the legacy proprietary <code>nvidia-580xx-dkms</code>, the older <code>nvidia-470xx-dkms</code>, or nouveau – and checks that the package it is about to recommend is present in the repository, reverting to nouveau when it is not."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.hardware.nvidia.p2
msgid "It then explains what it picked, and why."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.cpu.title
msgid "CPU vulnerabilities, detected and mitigated."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.cpu.p1
msgid "Spectre v2, Meltdown, MDS, TAA, MMIO Stale Data, RETBleed, SRSO, GDS, RFDS — Ditana reads <code>/sys/devices/system/cpu/vulnerabilities/</code> at install time, <a href=\"/under-the-hood/mitigations/\">enables only the mitigations your CPU actually needs</a>, and lets you review each one with documentation."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.cpu.p2
msgid "Every mitigation comes with its <em>cost</em> spelled out, so you can make an informed call instead of choosing between “secure default” and “fast default” blindly."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.cpu.alt
msgid "CPU vulnerability mitigation dialog showing detected vulnerabilities and selectable mitigations"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.hardening.alt
msgid "System hardening options dialog"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.hardening.title
msgid "System hardening, opt-out by default."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.hardening.body
msgid "Unprivileged user namespaces restricted to the programs that need them. Restricted ptrace. Hidden kernel pointers. Hardened FIFO and regular-file protection in sticky directories. SYN cookies, no ICMP redirects, no source routing, reverse-path filtering. Each option ships enabled, each one is explained – and developers get a profile that loosens the right things for debugging."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.installer.eyebrow
msgid "The installer itself"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.installer.title
msgid "Ten categories. Sensible defaults. No mystery."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.installer.lede
msgid "Tell the installer who you are – originating from a different distribution, migrating from KDE Plasma, Cinnamon, Xfce or MATE, working as a developer, running as a server, or falling into none of these – and it accordingly pre-flips checkboxes. You may examine and revise every single one prior to the start of installation."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.installer.categories.alt
msgid "Configuration categories overview dialog"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.installer.categories.title
msgid "One screen, the whole system."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.installer.categories.body
msgid "Configuration categories let you drill into desktops, browsers, file managers, office suites, AI tools, terminals, kernels, filesystems, hardening, mitigations, and the system allocator — all from one menu, all reversible, all explained."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.installer.impact.title
msgid "Every change comes with its impact."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.installer.impact.body
msgid "Toggle an option, see the consequence: which packages get added, which scripts will run, what other settings flip in response. The settings graph is data — Ditana shows you the path through it."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.installer.impact.alt
msgid "Settings impact overview showing dependent changes"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.config.eyebrow
msgctxt ".landing.config.eyebrow"
msgid "Configuration as data"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.config.title
msgid "The installer is small. The knowledge base is big."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.config.lede
msgid "A Linux installer should do more than partition, install packages, and hand off. PKGBUILDs only know about their own package — they can’t know what else is on the system. Ditana keeps the cross-cutting decisions in a separate, auditable knowledge base."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.config.kdl.title
msgid "Settings declare their dependencies in plain KDL."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.config.kdl.body
msgid "This excerpt says: install kitty <em>only</em> if the chosen terminal operates on Wayland alone <em>and</em> XFCE is also installed. Nobody would consider requesting that, and nobody needs to: the rule stands beside the package list, where a reviewer can see both the condition and the reason for it, rather than being hidden within installer code."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.config.browse
msgid "Browse ditana-config →"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.final.title
msgid "Ready to try it?"
msgstr ""

#: .landing.final.body
msgid "Ditana {{release}} is a {{size}} ISO with a detached GPG signature. It fetches its packages and its configuration while it installs, so a working network connection is part of the requirements. The full install takes 15–45 minutes depending on what you pick."
msgstr ""

#: .landing.final.whatsNew
msgid "What’s new in {{release}}"
msgstr ""
